":"Gjermundbu helmet: a rounded iron combat helmet with a smooth dome, a distinct 'spectacle' guard formed by a joined brow and nose strip creating an eye-and-nose frame, and no projections or attachments for horns. Render as solid iron color (hex #7A7E84) with a thin black outline and simple rivet dots along the lower edge only where the archaeological find indicates. Why recurring: the Gjermundbu helmet is the best-preserved authentic Viking-age helmet and is used as the visual counterpoint to the horn myth."},{"id":"plain_iron_helm_fragment","label":"Other Viking-age helmet fragment representation: plain iron dome fragments, sometimes with a short nose-guard strip or rivet holes, in dull steel-grey (hex #7A7E84). Always depicted broken or fragmentary on a labeled tray in museum/dig scenes. Why recurring: supports the narrative that surviving Viking helmets are sparse and utilitarian, not horned."},{"id":"museum_plinth_case","label":"Museum display case: low rectangular plinth in desaturated charcoal (hex #646464) with a clear glass box drawn as four thin outline lines and a small label plaque in light grey. Objects (helmets, fragments) sit centered on the plinth. Why recurring: several scenes show museum comparisons side-by-side (Veksø helmet vs Gjermundbu helmet)."},{"id":"theatre_horned_helmet","label":"Theatrical horned helmet: an exaggerated costume helmet used onstage — large, heavy-looking horns curving upward and outward, painted decorative metal finish in darkened brass (hex #AC8A3F) with large decorative rivet disks where horns attach. Proportions deliberately oversized for spectacle. Why recurring: represents 19th-century stage costuming (Doepler/Wagner) and clearly labelled as theatrical, not archaeological."}],"graphicLanguage":{"comparisonLayouts":"Three-panel horizontal sequence: (left) imagined horned Viking charge, (center) Bronze Age Veksø helmet close-up, (right) Gjermundbu helmet close-up. Each panel uses the same character scale to emphasize size/proportional differences.","timelineConventions":"A simple horizontal timeline bar across relevant scenes: left labeled 'Nordic Bronze Age ~1000 BCE' (bronze icon), middle blank gap labeled '— 1800+ years —', right labeled 'Viking Age ~800–1000 CE' (iron helmet icon). Use minimal ticks and two node labels only.","symbolicMetaphorLanguage":"Use the dramatic horned-helmet image as a repeated symbolic silhouette (a dark, oversized horned outline) whenever the narration refers to cultural myth/imagined image; contrast that silhouette with small, accurate objects on plinths to emphasize mismatch between myth and evidence."},"factualVisualConstraints":["Do not invent rivet holes or horn attachment hardware on the Gjermundbu or other authentic Viking-age helmets (archaeology shows none).","Veksø helmets must be represented as Bronze Age ceremonial bronze, not functional iron combat helmets.","Any depiction of organic materials (leather, antler) that are hypothesized to have rotted must be shown as speculative overlays (dashed lines or faded sketches) and labeled 'possible, but no surviving evidence'."]}

